<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">M. S. Subbulakshmi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1916">1916</a>–<a href="/years/2004">2004</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Madurai Shanmukhavadivu Subbulakshmi, M.S. Subbulakshmi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">M. S. Subbulakshmi was an Indian Carnatic singer from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> and in 1998 the first musician to be awarded the Bharat Ratna, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s highest civilian honour. The daughter of the veena player Shanmukhavadivu Ammal, of the devadasi community, she first performed in public in 1927 at the Rockfort Temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiruchirappalli">Tiruchirappalli</a> and sang at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> Music Academy in 1929, at 13. Her title role in the Tamil film Meera (1945) brought her national prominence. Her concerts abroad included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-general-assembly">United Nations General Assembly</a> in 1966, and in 1974 she received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-magsaysay">Ramon Magsaysay</a> Award. She stopped performing after her husband Kalki Sadasivam died in 1997, and died in Chennai in 2004.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/m-s-subbulakshmi.jpg" alt="M. S. Subbulakshmi" width="330" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Saregama Carnatic · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:M._S._Subbulakshmi_(03).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>M. S. Subbulakshmi</b>, Pandit Jasraj and Bhimsen Joshi sang it, Amitabh Bachchan recorded it with twenty other singers in 2011, and the American singer Krishna Das performs it in the West.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hanuman-chalisa">Hanuman Chalisa</a></li><li><blockquote>Mumbai radio played it in the 1940s, often sung by his daughter Mathuriben Khare; Vijay Bhatt's film Narsi Bhagat (1940), made at Gandhi's suggestion, ends with it; and <b>M. S. Subbulakshmi</b> sang it near the close of her Carnatic concerts.</blockquote><a href="/songs/vaishnava-jana-to">Vaishnava Jana To</a></li></ul></section>
